  "body": "Conjecture: The function open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object, whose documented rule says \"The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text\", gives the same answer for two replies that the rule requires it to tell apart. The first reply is the string consisting of three backticks, the word json, a newline, the text {\"a\":1} {\"x\":1}, a newline, three backticks, a newline, and the text {\"y\":1}. The second reply is the string consisting of three backticks, the word json, a newline, the text {\"a\":1} {\"z\":1}, a newline, three backticks, a newline, and the text {\"y\":1}. For the first reply, the rule requires the answer to be the object {\"x\":1}, because that is the last object inside the code fence. For the second reply, the rule requires the answer to be the object {\"z\":1}, because that is the last object inside the code fence. But the function returns the same answer for both: it returns the object {\"y\":1}, because its fenced pool treats the entire fenced content as one candidate, which fails to parse, and then it falls back to the last top-level object in the whole text, which is {\"y\":1} in both replies. I would be wrong if the function actually returned {\"x\":1} for the first reply and {\"z\":1} for the second, or if the documented rule were read as requiring the last fenced candidate that parses as a whole rather than the last object inside a fence."
}